00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.650
Let's get into it.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.960
Tell us a little bit
more about why you

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.632
decided to pick this pattern.

00:01:06.632 --> 00:01:07.590
POLONG LIN: Absolutely.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:10.590
So this pattern is all
around demand forecasting.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:15.030
And so whether you're a
consumer or business today,

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:17.100
and no matter where
you are in the world,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.250
you could go to a store, let's
say, hoping to make brownies,

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.150
and then realize that they're
out of chocolate powder, cocoa

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:24.870
powder.

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.540
And then, meanwhile, you
look in the grocery aisle,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.442
and you see sacks of potatoes.

00:01:29.442 --> 00:01:30.900
And they're starting
to turn green.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.460
They're starting to turn bad.

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.670
And this is kind of like the
essence of demand forecasting,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:40.410
is when businesses can try to
anticipate or sometimes not do

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:43.470
well in anticipating the
demands of the consumers.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.588
So in the case of
chocolate powder, maybe

00:01:45.588 --> 00:01:47.130
there wasn't enough
chocolate powder.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.860
They didn't quite
anticipate the demand

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:53.340
during this particular season
or particular day of the year.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.350
Whereas for potatoes,
they had a surplus.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.090
And so they had too
much of something.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.310
And so demand
forecasting is all about

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:01.920
trying to anticipate the
right amount of, let's say,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.660
product or item
that you might need,

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.590
or resources that
you might need.

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.970
So you really try to
save money on things

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.270
that you don't need to overly
supply, while at the same time

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.760
dedicating more of those
resources towards things

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.930
that you can anticipate will
have much higher demand,

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:19.860
essentially.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.970
BRUNO AZIZA: Typical
demand-and-supply problem.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.570
And I'm sure people are very
familiar with this problem

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:25.170
in retail.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:28.442
But this is also appropriate
for other industries, right?

00:02:28.442 --> 00:02:29.650
POLONG LIN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.110
There is, for example, energy.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.800
Energy providers have to try
to predict how much energy they

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:40.060
need to prepare on an
hourly basis, I assume.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:42.010
And there's obviously
daily cycles.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.240
So during the day versus
during the evening,

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.240
people use energy
differently, their electricity

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.907
at home differently
when they're cooking

00:02:47.907 --> 00:02:50.530
versus when they're outside,
or, I guess, nowadays, when most

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.457
people are at home as well.

00:02:52.457 --> 00:02:54.040
And then there's
also seasonal cycles,

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.890
because when it gets colder,
like it is now here in London,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.060
people may tend to use a
little bit more energy.

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:02.140
So energy providers have
to really think about that.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.320
And it's not just about energy
that could be mobile apps

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.080
or telecom or online
gaming services,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.370
where if you have
a lot of users,

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:13.030
and they suddenly
come to your service

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:16.030
because the holidays or
a weekend or something,

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:19.540
and you don't have the right
resources allocated in place,

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.430
that could disrupt the
service for everyone.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.460
So it's not just not being
able to make brownies.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:26.860
It's, no one can make brownies.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.000
No one can enjoy brownies.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.030
Or essentially, translating it
back to that but your domain,

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:35.080
no one can enjoy the service
because, let's say, the servers

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:36.560
are down.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.010
And so for that reason,
demand forecasting

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.470
can really span across
lots of industries

00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:46.010
and be really impactful to
the end consumer as well.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.140
BRUNO AZIZA: So very relevant
to retail, of course,

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.330
energy, telco.

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:51.478
But this problem of
demand and supply

00:03:51.478 --> 00:03:53.020
is present across
the entire economy.

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:55.540
And that's why,
with this solution,

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.183
we know we're going to
help many, many customers.

00:03:58.183 --> 00:04:00.100
In fact, a lot of these
design patterns-- they

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.750
originate from customer demand.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:04.780
So tell us a little
bit about how

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:06.970
this is going to impact
the customer deployment

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.612
and how you've seen
this in action.

00:04:09.612 --> 00:04:10.820
POLONG LIN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.510
So you can do demand
forecasting today

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:15.070
in a variety of different ways.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.310
You could use different kinds
of technologies and softwares

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.050
and programming languages.

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:22.120
This demand forecasting
pattern focuses

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:26.320
on using, training, deploying
demand forecasting models

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:27.790
with BigQuery ML.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:30.220
And so if you think about,
to kind of take a step back

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:33.350
and think about how it machine
learning models typically

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.520
get used in production,
traditionally, a business

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:38.950
may have their data in,
let's say, a data warehouse.

00:04:38.950 --> 00:04:42.340
And they often end up extracting
that data out of the data

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:46.090
warehouse to train and deploy
the machine learning models.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.560
But this can run into
some difficulties.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.700
So if you're extracting data
from a data warehouse, number

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:54.220
one, that's a data
governance issue sometimes.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:57.070
If you have any sort of PII data
coming out of a data warehouse,

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.570
you now have to track where all
your data is going and worrying

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:02.410
about data leakage.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:03.650
That's one thing.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.310
And then the second
thing is around

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:09.040
provisioning the right resources
to train your machine learning

00:05:09.040 --> 00:05:11.740
model and finding a place to
host your machine learning

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:15.070
model as well, trying to deploy
your machine learning model.

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:19.930
And these are all complexities
that can really bog down

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:21.310
a machine learning pipeline.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:25.000
It can make things quite a
bit longer or more complex

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.200
for you to go from zero to a
fully productionized machine

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:31.330
learning pipeline very quickly.

00:05:31.330 --> 00:05:34.240
And that's where BigQuery
ML really fills in this gap.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.540
So BigQuery, which is a fully
managed, serverless data

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:40.180
warehouse-- you know, you
have your data storage,

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.400
let's say, all your
transactional data directly

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.840
in BigQuery.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.540
You could do your
analytics also in BigQuery,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:47.925
and it's very easy to do so.

00:05:47.925 --> 00:05:49.300
And now with
BigQuery ML, you can

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.160
do all your machine
learning workloads,

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:53.527
train and deploy your machine
learning models directly

00:05:53.527 --> 00:05:54.235
with BigQuery ML.

00:05:54.235 --> 00:05:57.933
So you have this kind of
all-in-one sort of thing.

00:05:57.933 --> 00:05:59.350
By that nature,
it means you don't

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.850
have to worry about
data governance,

00:06:00.850 --> 00:06:02.770
and you don't have to
worry about finding

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:06.190
some extra infrastructure,
exporting, finding

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.860
some other service to be able
to train and deploy your models.

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:12.190
So that's where BigQuery ML
fits into demand forecasting--

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:16.330
making things a lot easier for
customers who go from months

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.850
to creating a machine
learning pipeline down to,

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.920
let's say, weeks, or
weeks down to days.

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:25.828
So an order of magnitude
faster by using BigQuery ML.

00:06:25.828 --> 00:06:28.120
BRUNO AZIZA: So you have the
benefits of the governance

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:29.680
issues you don't
have to worry about,

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:32.560
and the time to
market, if you will,

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:34.120
because data doesn't
have to move.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:37.180
It's integrated
into the same suite.

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:38.673
That's a great overview.

00:06:38.673 --> 00:06:40.090
Tell us-- if I'm
listening to you,

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:41.715
and I want to start
using this pattern,

00:06:41.715 --> 00:06:43.242
what do I do next here?

00:06:43.242 --> 00:06:44.200
POLONG LIN: Absolutely.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.050
So you can explore the
pattern, and Bruno will point

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:50.170
to this outside of the video.

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:53.570
But the pattern will essentially
give you a bit of a quick start

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.890
into figuring how to do this
in your own environments

00:06:56.890 --> 00:06:57.920
very quickly.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:00.400
So starting off with the
sample retail data set

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.780
that you can replace
with your own data set--

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.780
so we have this
sample to start with,

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:06.280
and then essentially
showing you how

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:08.890
to train that model
with BigQuery ML.

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:11.560
This is a time series
demand forecasting model

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:13.510
that you'd be using
with BigQuery ML.

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.930
It's very easy, just not
too many lines of code.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.640
And you have a model.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.030
And then number 3, then
how to make your forecasts

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:24.250
once the model is trained
within BigQuery ML.

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:26.410
And again, this is
all within BigQuery.

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:29.130
You don't have to exit
the ecosystem at all.

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:31.010
And then finally, once
you have your models,

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:33.800
you have your
forecast maybe running

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.260
on a daily, weekly
basis, you also

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:39.500
want to make sure your models
are updated and staying fresh

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:42.650
so that with new data
coming in, you're constantly

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:44.450
updating the models
that you really

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:46.820
can forecast and
anticipate demand based off

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:47.960
of recent data.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:50.650
So I show you how to do
that in the pattern as well.

00:07:50.650 --> 00:07:51.900
BRUNO AZIZA: That's fantastic.

